WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:13.400
[Fred Margulies] I attended a, uh, Jewish
school—Adass Yisroel in Berlin,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.780
as did my sister.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:19.880
Most of the time we were very aware of the
fact that we live in dangerous times.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:27.440
On Kol Nidre before we went to synagogue,
it's the custom for father to bless his children.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:35.080
And I remember all the years that I can think
back that when my father blessed my sister

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.710
and me,

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.220
he always cried.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.300
And I assumed that

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.940
it was

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.140
because they knew.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.260
Small as you were, you—you realized that.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:57.600
And, of course, we were confronted daily,
daily,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.100
with a swastika

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.720
and the hordes,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.880
and, in the glass cases on the street corners,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.140
the display of the Stürmer.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.680
My father would sometimes pick one up in a
train and bring it home.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.860
So there was this feeling of

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.040
an impending doom.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.700
He was taken away very early in the morning.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.560
My mother was crying, and there was such to-do

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.940
that my sister and I woke up, and we went
to see what was going on.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.580
And, uh, I remember the policeman saying,
as far as he knows,

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.299
that they're being deported to Poland.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:50.040
And, um, that's the last time I saw my father.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.380
When Kristallnacht came, it was a—a disaster.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.600
And it became clear that life for Jews in
Germany

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.760
was... all over.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.700
Decisions had to be made as to who should
go and who should not go.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.700
For example, one of my uncles and aunt 
decided not to send both of their children.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.110
They would send one child to test the waters

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.700
and keep one child with them

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.760
to see if this would work, because there was
no certainty.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:32.860
It was actually fraught with danger.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:36.620
And we were made aware of the fact that we
will be carrying a luggage tag on our lapel,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.880
and all it would say is the name and the district
in Berlin which we lived—

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.460
no street, nothing else, no identity.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.580
And that we were to act as though we didn't
know from where we came.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.460
We left in the morning, early in the morning.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.730
It was timed so that we would get to the border

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.720
at night, because we needed the cover of darkness,
of course.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.720
I remember leaving from Bahnhof Zoo,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.040
and I remember that not—

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.300
about a minute or two out of Bahnhof Zoo,

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.580
on the elevated tracks of the railroad,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.140
came into view the hull

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.680
of this burned and destroyed

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.840
famous synagogue of Berlin, Fasanenstrasse.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.840
And that was the last

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.460
stab in the heart for a little Jewish boy,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:35.340
to see the burnt hulk of this famous synagogue.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.780
I also remember very well that my mother took
me into a corner in this railroad compartment,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.300
I remember well what she said to me,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.260
in order to make sure I understood, because,

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.920
of course, I was a little boy very attached
to my mother.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.280
It was a great love relationship.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.180
In that little speech in the train,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.340
I always say I became bar mitzvah.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.750
I have not been formally bar mitzvahed.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.970
When I was 13, I was not bar mitzvahed.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.080
I was bar mitzvahed when I was 11.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.820
By my mother. No minyan, no rabbi.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.940
I was given my—my orders, my commandments

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.360
as to what to do.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.860
And I remember that.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.320
I was to be the hero of the family—

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.980
tapfer zu sein—to be courageous.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.840
And so the journey took—took its last hour
or two.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.040
At the station, as we got off the train, uh,

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.159
my mother said to me, you must not try—

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.760
you must—must not turn around and look back,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.860
and you must not cry.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.600
And she did not kiss me good luck—goodbye,

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.370
because there was just no time.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.880
We went to the left, we children.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.080
And I remember my mother and the other lady
turned to the right.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.340
And that was the last we saw of them.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.040
That was December the 6th, 1938,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:57.980
in the middle of the night.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.259
I remember having to go to a photographer

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.980
in Hoogeveen to have pictures taken.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.160
I still have those pictures in the little
Dutch folder at home.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.040
And, uh, we got to England.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.960
And that was, of course, the last I saw of
my sister, who unfortunately perished in Bergen-Belsen.

